gs but these are things that  are habits  that slavic speakers have and which i as  an english speaker don't have and so how  do i then  develop these habits  and i because i'm going to be in  ukraine speak initially in eastern  ukraine and kiev where they mostly speak  russian and then in levi where they  speak  ukrainian  what i want to do is make a concerted  effort to improve not so much to expand  my vocabulary  but to improve in the nuts and bolts  when i first started into reading up on  language learning  somebody described language learning as  consisting of two strategies  one is the sort of  big picture learning and the other is  the nuts and bolts  and so big picture is lots of listening  and reading interesting content  eventually  acquiring more vocabulary  gaining familiarity with the language  but at some point you also have to work  on the nuts and bolts i have found from  experience that reading declension  tables reading explanations really  doesn't do much for me i don't retain  very much so i still struggle with my  accuracy and so what my strategy now is  to use our mini stories and i'm doing  that because i've had so much success in  learning greek and and persian and  arabic using the mini stories i feel  there are they are a very good way of  focusing in on the nuts and bolts  because what happens is you know the  story i know the stories i've done them  now in three four languages i know the  stories i've listened to them many many  times so i'm better able to focus in on  the nuts and bolts  uh i be i'm aware of different aspects  of the language every time i hear  the story or listen or read the story  plus  in link i can i can save phrases i'm  only for example i now in my russian and  ukrainian i only save phrases phrases  that embody these aspects of the  language that i want to get better at  then as i turn the page in my lesson i  review these  structures  and i only use the reverse flash card so  i see it in english i have to say it